Pumpkin Curried Hummus
  --Nasim Lahbichi

15oz can of chickpeas, aquafaba aka bean juice reserved
4-6 tbsp of aquafaba (or water), ensure it is COLD
2 tbsp lemon juice, juice from ~1/2 a lemon
1 tsp lemon zest
2 garlic cloves, minced or grated 
3/4 c pumpkin puree
3 tbsp tahini 
1 tbsp evoo
1/2 tbsp curry powder
1/2 tsp salt (to start, add more to taste)
1/2 tsp ground cumin
1/2 tsp sannam chili or cayenne pepper 
1 tsp baking soda (this will help achieve a smooth hummus)
Sweet & Salty Curry Coated Pumpkin Seeds,  for garnish 
olive oil, for garnish 
chopped cilantro, for garnish 
sprinkle of chili/cayenne, for garnish

Begin by draining the chickpeas and RESERVE THE AQUAFABA (aka bean juice). Place the aquafaba in a container in the freezer to chill for later use in this recipe. o a food processor or high-speed blender, add chickpeas, lemon juice, pumpkin puree, tahini, olive oil, salt, curry powder, cumin, chili, and baking soda. (Baking soda will help the hummus become more smooth by softening the chickpeas during processing) Blend until smooth. If too thick, you will need to add chilled aquafaba or water to the mixture to help smoothen it out. Start with adding 1-2 tbsp of chilled aquafaba (which will help “fluff” up the hummus) or water until desired consistency. (4-5 tbsp of additional liquid works best). Once smooth, pour into a dish. Drizzle a heaping of fresh, good-quality evoo. 


Notes: You can choose to use dried chickpeas that have been soaked overnight and boiled, but for ease and our sanity, this recipe works wonderfully using the canned stuff! Folks suggest less baking soda as flavor overpowers. Also add garlic
